Close the /proc/<pid>/attr/* and setxattr(security.selinux) hidden-type oracles.

The RICHER of the two attr variants, and NOT a superset-by-accident: it guards
selinux_inode_setxattr() as well as selinux_lsm_setattr(), so
`setxattr(p, "security.selinux", "u:object_r:ksu_file:s0")` -- a second,
independent one-syscall probe of the same bit -- is covered too. The fallback
hide_selinux_attr.patch guards only the setattr path. Prefer this one wherever
it applies.

Both guards sit AFTER the stock avc check (FILE__RELABELFROM, and the per-attr
PROCESS__SET* check respectively) and immediately before
security_context_to_sid(), so a domain lacking the permission still gets the
stock EACCES and never EINVAL. See common/_hook/README.md for why that ordering
is the whole design and what breaks when it is inverted.

FIXED HERE: the match was `strnstr(value, ":ksu:", size)` over the raw buffer.
security_context_to_sid() takes the same @size but parses it as a C string and
stops at the first NUL, so "u:r:untrusted_app:s0\0:ksu:" answered EINVAL here and
OK on stock, while ":kzu:" in the same position answered OK on both. Stock cannot
make that distinction, so the cloak was the tell. Both scans are now bounded by
strnlen(value, size) -- exactly the bytes the parser will look at. Same fix as
selinuxfs.c's sel_ctx_hidden(), for the same reason.

APPLIES TO

android16-6.12 (6.12.90) only. Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` there and
confirmed to FAIL on 5.10 / 5.15 / 6.1 / 6.6: 6.12 renamed the setattr path to
selinux_lsm_setattr() and reshaped the per-attr avc block into a switch. 6.6 has
the mnt_idmap-era selinux_inode_setxattr() but still the 6.6-shaped
selinux_setprocattr(), so it takes the fallback and keeps the setxattr gap
recorded in the README. security/selinux/hooks.o compiles clean (ARCH=arm64
LLVM=1). Generated by editing the real file and running `diff -up`.
